Frozenness: value is a single numeric literal or a string that is a single numeric literal. Also treat e.g. "s = 3" as fixed value (has a number and no comparator/interval). Detect comparators: >, <, ∈, in (, ranges with comma/interval. I'll implement:
```python
NUM_RE = re.compile(r"^\s*[-+]?\d+(?:\.\d+)?\s*$")
BOUNDED_RE = re.compile(r"[<>≤≥]|\bin\b|\(|\)|\[|\]|,|interval|range", re.I)
def frozen(value): 
    v=norm(value)
    if NUM_RE.match(v): return True
    # form "s = 3" or "3"
    m=re.fullmatch(r"[A-Za-z_]+\s*=\s*([-+]?\d+(?:\.\d+)?)", v)
    return bool(m)
```
Careful: "s > 5/2" contains > and / → not frozen. "delta in (1/2, 1)" → not frozen.

